            <ArticleTitle>A piece of glass in the heart.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>335-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Most posttraumatic foreign bodies are metal objects in the heart, such as bullets and needles; nonmetal objects are very rare. We now report a case in which a piece of glass entered a girl's heart from an injury to the right side of her neck. Six months later, we successfully removed the glass shard from the anterior wall of the right ventricle.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
